WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
Welcome to Grade 6 English, Unit 1: There's a Lesson in That.

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:11.560
In this unit you will explore the world of fables —
short stories that teach us important lessons about life.

3
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:18.560
Fables have been told for thousands of years, passed
from generation to generation by storytellers, teachers,

4
00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:19.500
and parents.

5
00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:27.560
They often use animal characters or simple human figures to
show us truths about honesty, courage, kindness, and wisdom.

6
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:32.880
As you work through this lesson, you will
read a fable, answer questions about it,

7
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.580
learn an important grammar rule
called the Present Perfect Tense,

8
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:40.220
and even write your own fable with a clear moral message.

9
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.860
Every section builds on the one before it,
so take your time and enjoy the journey.

10
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:52.640
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

11
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.640
By the end of this unit, you will be able to explain
what a fable is and identify its key features,

12
00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.240
including characters, setting, problem, and moral.

13
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.400
You will also read a fable closely and
identify the moral or lesson it teaches.

14
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.880
You will also use vocabulary related
to governance, justice, community,

15
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.820
and storytelling confidently in your own sentences.

16
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:19.740
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

17
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:30.040
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: accommodate,
accommodation, amateur, attached, protagonist, admittedly.

18
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:35.860
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

19
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.620
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

20
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:47.380
The reading sequence includes The Weaver and the
Wind, part 1 and The Weaver and the Wind, part 2.

21
00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:48.880
Read actively.

22
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:53.980
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

23
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.120
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

24
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.540
The first language focus is What
the Present Perfect Tense Shows.

25
00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.700
The present perfect links the past to the present.

26
00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:14.920
We use it for life experiences, recent news, and
situations that began earlier and still matter now,

27
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.440
without saying exactly when they happened.

28
00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:24.580
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

29
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.540
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Retell a Fable from Memory.

30
00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:34.260
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

31
00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.180
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

32
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.940
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

33
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:43.680
You are ready to begin.

34
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.960
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

35
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.900
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

36
00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:01.880
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
